<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Guelders</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper Guelders was the southern quarter of the Duchy of Guelders, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Unlike the northern quarters, it stayed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> it was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and its lands now lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Limburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-guelders.jpg" alt="Upper Guelders" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original map: de:Velhagen &amp; Klasing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gelre1477.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>From the 11th century the counts, later dukes, of Guelders gathered territories along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, which were cut off from their later lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> by the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, a long-standing enemy. These original lands formed Upper Guelders, one of the duchy's four quarters; the other three, Zutphen County, the Veluwe Quarter and the Nijmegen Quarter, lay to the north and now make up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>. Its chief towns were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a>, and with Cleves it belonged to the Kleverlandish dialect area of Low Franconian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1471 Duke Arnold of Guelders, in conflict with his son Adolf of Egmond, won the support of the Burgundian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> at the price of pawning his duchy, and Charles seized it on Arnold's death in 1473. Arnold's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders">Charles of Egmond</a> regained it in 1492 with help from the local nobility and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a>, but left no heirs and bequeathed it to Duke William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> in 1538. William renounced it in 1543 in favour of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, who joined it to the Seventeen Provinces and passed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> in 1556.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> the three lower quarters joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> in 1579, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a> and the upper quarter stayed in Spanish hands, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-munster">Peace of Münster</a> confirmed in 1648; hence the name Spanish Guelders. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 most of it, Geldern included, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and Erkelenz to the Duchy of Jülich, while the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a> remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1790.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the Final Act of the Vienna Congress in 1815, the western part of Prussian Upper Guelders and the former Austrian part became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of Limburg, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> kept the rest, which joined its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> in 1822. The line drawn then still forms the Dutch-German border.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Guelders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Dutch Revolt it split: the northern quarters joined the Dutch Republic, while <b>Upper Guelders</b> around Roermond stayed Spanish.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Duchy of Guelders</a></li></ul></section>
